  "summary": "Devil's-claw black on yucca white — desert baskets woven into museum grade.",
  "description": "Tohono O'odham (Papago) basketry coils bear-grass or yucca with devil's-claw seed pods split into black lace for figures: whirling logs, eagles, butterflies, and especially the man-in-the-maze lid design — a spiral of a traveler's life path. Utility coiling fed the desert economy for centuries; the 20th-century revival (Annie Antone, Terrol Dew Johnson) pushed tightly-woven polychrome forms into fine-art galleries, with weavers harvesting and preparing every material from the Sonoran Desert themselves. The tradition is one of the strongest living basket lineages in North America, taught in basketry organizations like TOCA.",
  "culturalContext": "Tohono O'odham basketry is desert knowledge made object: weavers harvest and process every material themselves, the man-in-the-maze lid encodes O'odham life-path philosophy, and artists like Annie Antone and Terrol Dew Johnson carried coiling into fine-art galleries while TOCA keeps the tradition a community economy.",
